IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `uc-core`

-- The Universal Credit Regulations 2013 (S.I. 2013/376), piece uc1: helpers for counting that several of uc1's modules share.
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2013-376.txt, a plain-text rendering of the revised text (legislation.gov.uk, version 2026-07-16)
-- made by a converter script. It is not the official text. Every `-- src:N | ...` line copies line N of that file.
-- Target: the stable build 1.6.98. Seat: a person who claims or receives Universal Credit, alone or as one of a couple.
--
-- WHAT IS HERE: no provision of the text is encoded in this module. It holds three small pieces of arithmetic that the
-- text takes for granted and that more than one regulation of uc1 needs: an age in complete years on a date, the last day of
-- a calendar month, and the comparison of an absence with a limit ("expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months").
-- Each rule names the line of the text that needs it. Names begin `uc1 —`, which keeps them apart from the other pieces' names.
--
-- COUNTING CONVENTION for an absence (fork F-ABS in NOTES.md, assumed and not ruled): an absence that began on a day lasts
-- longer than N months if the day to which it has lasted is later than the day N months after it began (31 August plus 6 months
-- is 28 February, by the core). An absence that ends on the day N months after it began does not exceed N months.

-- Age in complete years on a date. The text speaks of "the age of 16" and "aged under 25" without saying how an age is
-- counted; the usual count of complete years is taken (fork F-AGE in NOTES.md). A birthday on 29 February is reached in a
-- year that is not a leap year on 1 March, because the month and day are compared.
-- src:160 | (1) A person who has reached the age of 16 but not the age of 20 is a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 1 of the Act and these Regulations—
@ref reg 5(1) (src:160)
GIVEN `date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the date on which the age is asked` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`uc1 — the age in complete years on a date` `date of birth` `the date on which the age is asked` MEANS
    ((DATE_YEAR `the date on which the age is asked`) MINUS (DATE_YEAR `date of birth`))
    MINUS (IF (((DATE_MONTH `the date on which the age is asked`) TIMES 100) PLUS (DATE_DAY `the date on which the age is asked`))
                LESS THAN (((DATE_MONTH `date of birth`) TIMES 100) PLUS (DATE_DAY `date of birth`))
           THEN 1
           ELSE 0)

-- A leap year, which regulation 21(2)(b) names ("or, in a leap year, the 28th day").
-- src:436 | (b) if the first date of entitlement falls on the 29th or 30th day of a month, each assessment period begins on the 29th or 30th day of the month (as above) except in February when it begins on the 27th day or, in a leap year, the 28th day.
@ref reg 21(2)(b) (src:436)
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc1 — a leap year` year MEANS
    ((year MODULO 4) EQUALS 0)
    AND ((NOT ((year MODULO 100) EQUALS 0)) OR ((year MODULO 400) EQUALS 0))

-- The last day of a calendar month, as a day of the month (28 to 31). Regulation 21(2)(a) needs it ("the last day of the month").
-- src:435 | (a) if the first date of entitlement falls on the 31st day of a month, each assessment period begins on the last day of the month; and
@ref reg 21(2)(a) (src:435)
GIVEN month IS A NUMBER
      year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`uc1 — the last day of a calendar month` month year MEANS
    DATE_DAY ((Date 1 (month PLUS 1) year) MINUS 1)

-- An absence from a person's household or from Great Britain, as the text measures it: when it began, the day to which it has
-- lasted (the day the question is asked, if the person is still away; otherwise the day the person came back) and the day on
-- which it is expected to end.
-- src:135 | (6) Where the claimant is a member of a couple, and the other member is temporarily absent from the claimant's household, they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months.
-- src:289 | (i) the absence is not expected to exceed, and does not exceed, one month, or
@ref reg 3(6) (src:135); reg 11(1)(b)(i) (src:289)
DECLARE `Absence that is measured against a limit` HAS
    `first day of the absence`                IS A DATE
    `day to which the absence has lasted`     IS A DATE
    `expected last day of the absence`        IS A DATE

-- The absence has lasted longer than the period that ends on a given day (the limit day).
-- src:135 | (6) Where the claimant is a member of a couple, and the other member is temporarily absent from the claimant's household, they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months.
@ref reg 3(6) (src:135)
GIVEN absence IS AN `Absence that is measured against a limit`
      `limit day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc1 — the absence has lasted beyond a day` absence `limit day` MEANS
    (absence's `day to which the absence has lasted`) GREATER THAN `limit day`

-- The absence is expected to last beyond the limit day.
-- src:135 | (6) Where the claimant is a member of a couple, and the other member is temporarily absent from the claimant's household, they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months.
@ref reg 3(6) (src:135)
GIVEN absence IS AN `Absence that is measured against a limit`
      `limit day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc1 — the absence is expected to last beyond a day` absence `limit day` MEANS
    (absence's `expected last day of the absence`) GREATER THAN `limit day`

-- "expected to exceed, or does exceed" a time limit counted from the first day of the absence.
-- src:135 | (6) Where the claimant is a member of a couple, and the other member is temporarily absent from the claimant's household, they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months.
@ref reg 3(6) (src:135)
GIVEN absence IS AN `Absence that is measured against a limit`
      limit IS A `Time limit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc1 — the absence is expected to exceed or does exceed a time limit` absence limit MEANS
    (`uc1 — the absence is expected to last beyond a day` absence
         (`core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date` (absence's `first day of the absence`) limit))
    OR (`uc1 — the absence has lasted beyond a day` absence
         (`core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date` (absence's `first day of the absence`) limit))

-- "not expected to exceed, and does not exceed" a time limit counted from the first day of the absence.
-- src:289 | (i) the absence is not expected to exceed, and does not exceed, one month, or
@ref reg 11(1)(b)(i) (src:289)
GIVEN absence IS AN `Absence that is measured against a limit`
      limit IS A `Time limit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc1 — the absence is not expected to exceed and does not exceed a time limit` absence limit MEANS
    NOT (`uc1 — the absence is expected to exceed or does exceed a time limit` absence limit)

-- The 1st September following a birthday (regulation 5(1)(a) and (b) name the 16th and the 19th; regulation 33(1)(a)(ii) the 16th).
-- A birthday that falls on or after 1 September has its following 1 September in the next calendar year, and one that falls
-- on 1 September itself is not followed by that same 1 September (fork F-5-SEPT, assumed and not ruled). Only the month and the
-- day are compared, so a birth on 29 February needs no special case.
-- src:161 | (a) up to, but not including, the 1st September following their 16th birthday; and
@ref reg 5(1)(a) (src:161)
GIVEN `date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the birthday reached` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`uc1 — the 1st September following a birthday` `date of birth` `the birthday reached` MEANS
    Date 1 9
        ((DATE_YEAR `date of birth`)
         PLUS `the birthday reached`
         PLUS (IF (((DATE_MONTH `date of birth`) TIMES 100) PLUS (DATE_DAY `date of birth`)) LESS THAN 901 THEN 0 ELSE 1))
